@startuml name
title Project 06 — Mutex (exp2) vs Atomic fetch_add (exp3) on One Shared Counter

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam maxMessageSize 160

participant "Thread A\n(pinned CPU 2)" as A
participant "Thread B\n(pinned CPU 4)" as B
participant "Shared counter\n(one cache line)" as C
participant "Kernel\n(futex)" as K

== exp2_mutex: std::lock_guard<std::mutex> ==

A -> C: lock() — CAS on mutex state
activate C
C --> A: acquired
A -> C: ++v (plain add, lock already held)
A -> C: unlock() — release store
deactivate C

note right of B: lock already taken by A
B -> C: lock() — CAS fails, marks "contended"
B -> K: futex_wait()
note over K: often returns immediately —\nA may have already unlocked
A -> K: unlock() sees "contended" flag,\ncalls futex_wake() regardless
K --> B: woken
B -> C: lock() retried — succeeds
activate C
C --> B: acquired
B -> C: ++v
B -> C: unlock()
deactivate C

note over A, B
  measured: 24.55 ns/increment, 37% of thread CPU time in kernel,
  ~60 voluntary context switches per 10M-increment trial
  — despite that, threads almost never actually slept long
end note

== exp3_atomic: std::atomic<uint64_t>::fetch_add ==

A -> C: lock add $1 — RMW, core takes line Exclusive
activate C
C --> A: done (one instruction, no lock object, no syscall)
deactivate C

note right of B: wants the same line,\nwaits only for hardware ownership transfer
B -> C: lock add $1 — RMW, line transferred to B's core
activate C
C --> B: done
deactivate C

note over A, B
  measured: 8.79 ns/increment, 0% kernel time, 0 context switches
  — ~2.8x faster than the mutex, same correctness guarantee
end note

@enduml
